# ABOUTME: Declares Tie::Hash's methods; a bare `use Tie::Hash` installs nothing.
# ABOUTME: Measured on perl 5.42.0 with Tie::Hash 1.06: no method carries a prototype.
package Tie::Hash;

# Every name below is a method, so each takes an invocant and nothing is
# marked :export: measured on 5.42.0, `use Tie::Hash;` installs nothing
# in the caller.
#
# Tie::Hash is an abstract base meant to be subclassed (perltie). EXISTS
# is documented as "a stub that simply croaks", and measured to:
# unconditionally, on Tie::Hash itself and on every subclass. new,
# TIEHASH and CLEAR are different: each is written to work when reached
# through a subclass that overrides its counterpart, not only to croak
# (measured, scratchpad/audit-C.md finding 34) --
#   - new calls $pkg->TIEHASH(@_), which a subclass defining only
#     TIEHASH dispatches to its own override: My->new returns a blessed
#     My, not a death, when My defines TIEHASH but not new.
#   - TIEHASH itself falls back to $pkg->new(@_) when $pkg defines its
#     own new (and is not Tie::Hash itself, which would recurse): N->TIEHASH
#     returns a blessed N when N defines new but not TIEHASH.
#   - CLEAR calls $self->FIRSTKEY/NEXTKEY/DELETE in a loop with no
#     explicit return; on a subclass defining all three it completes and
#     answers '' (measured), not a death.
# Called directly on Tie::Hash itself, with no override anywhere in the
# chain -- the only call the return-type oracle's generic bare-class
# mechanism can make -- all three still croak; each is exempt from it
# for that reason (ooExempt), and held instead by
# TestTieHashInheritedMethodsReturn, which measures the subclass-dispatch
# paths above directly.
sub new (Str $class: List @args) Object;
sub TIEHASH (Str $class: List @args) Object;
# EXISTS and CLEAR are instance methods (perltie: "EXISTS this, key",
# "CLEAR this"), not class ones.
sub EXISTS (Object $self: Str $key) None;
sub CLEAR (Object $self:) Any;

# Tie::StdHash and Tie::ExtraHash (audit-C.md finding 35) are
# separate packages in Tie/Hash.pm, not this one, and this declaration
# format holds one package per file; declaring either needs its own
# .tpm and a batch entry (perltest.OOModules or similar), which is
# deferred rather than done as part of this fix.
